          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is osteoarthritis not simply “wear and tear” of a joint?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is more than simple wear and tear. The source describes it as a disorder of the whole joint, including the cartilage, the bone underneath, the ligaments, the joint lining, and the surrounding muscles. Cartilage is gradually lost, and the bone beneath it reshapes itself in response. (*Source: Osteoarthritis, general clinical reference*)\n\nThe source also says the process is active rather than purely mechanical. It involves inflammation and attempted repair. The \"wear and tear\" idea can lead people to rest the joint, but the source notes that muscle weakness from reduced use can make the joint worse. For that reason, exercise is described as a first-line treatment, not just an add-on. (*Source: Osteoarthritis, general clinical reference*)\n\nThe passage I retrieved does not explain the specific biological steps of inflammation or repair in detail. For more on those, or on how the condition applies to your own situation, a clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
